description:
crate-jmap-tasks-server/src/memory.rs:81-91:

    /// A simple string error for \`MemoryBackend\` failures.
    #[derive(Debug)]
    pub struct MemoryError(pub String);

The pub String field is part of the public API. Once this ships in 0.1.x, callers can:

  - Match on it: \`if let MemoryError(msg) = err { ... }\`
  - Construct it directly: \`MemoryError(\"foo\".to_owned())\`
  - Use it in pattern bindings: \`let MemoryError(msg) = err;\`

Each of these locks the maintainer out of evolving the error to structured form. Realistic future shapes that all become breaking changes:

    pub struct MemoryError { msg: String, kind: ErrorKind }
    pub struct MemoryError { msg: Cow<'static, str> }
    pub enum MemoryError { Deserialize(String), Storage(String), ... }

All three are common refactors when a backend error type grows. None is reachable from the current shape without a breaking change.

The trait it implements (std::error::Error, Display, Debug) only requires opaqueness. The pub field gives consumers more access than the trait contract needs. Conventional fix:

    pub struct MemoryError {
        msg: String,
    }

    impl MemoryError {
        pub fn new(msg: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
            Self { msg: msg.into() }
        }
        pub fn message(&self) -> &str {
            &self.msg
        }
    }

This preserves the existing Display impl (uses self.msg), keeps construction available via ::new(), exposes the message via a getter, and leaves room for adding kind/source fields without breaking callers.

Also relevant: the constructor pattern (MemoryError(format!(...))) is used throughout memory.rs (lines 365, 619, 627, 680, 769, 778). Internal usage migrates to MemoryError::new(format!(...)) — minor diff.

The MemoryBackend module-level doc already disclaims API stability (\"may break across minor versions while the crate is pre-1.0\"). Even so, fixing this BEFORE 0.2 is cheaper than after.

Workspace propagation: this also applies to sibling backend error types. A quick check shows the canonical mail-server MemoryBackend may have the same pattern — recommend a workspace-wide sweep bead before the propagation rule catches it.
